4. Routes (routes/web.php)
Public:

Method	URI	Action	Name
GET	/	closure → welcome	
GET	/page/{slug}	PageController@show	pages.show
GET	/posts	PostController@publicIndex	posts.index
GET	/posts/{slug}	PostController@publicShow	posts.show
GET	/category/{slug}	PostController@publicByCategory	categories.show
Admin (group: middleware('auth'), prefix('admin'), name('admin.')):

Resource: pages, posts, categories

POST posts/upload-image → PostController@uploadImage (name admin.posts.upload_image)

Menu: GET/POST/PUT/DELETE /menu

Settings: GET/PUT /settings

Breeze:

GET /dashboard → redirect to admin.pages.index

/profile — inline closures for edit/update/destroy

/auth/* — from routes/auth.php

5. Data model
Model	Table	Columns used by app	Notes
Page	pages	title, slug, content, is_published, is_admin_only	casts booleans
Post	posts	title, body, is_published, image_path, category_id	no slug column
Category	categories	name, slug	hasMany posts
MenuItem	menu_items	title, url, page_id, order	belongsTo Page; accessor target_url
Setting	settings	key, value	key-value store
User	users	standard Breeze	
Post has no slug column. publicShow() compensates by computing slug from title at read time. PostController::store()/update() still compute a slug value, but it's silently dropped because it's not in $fillable. Stable slugs would need a migration.

6. Layouts
Two parallel layouts. This is the main source of confusion in the project.

layouts/admin.blade.php
Used by @extends('layouts.admin') + @section('content')

Contains the settings @php block that reads $globalSettings (or queries Setting::pluck) and defines CSS variables

Renders @include('layouts.navigation'), then @yield('content'), then @stack('scripts')

Contains CKEditor .ck-content styling and admin-panel-specific CSS (button[type="submit"] uses --primary-color)

layouts/app.blade.php
Used via <x-app-layout> (App\View\Components\AppLayout)

Same settings @php block

Uses {{ $slot }} — not @yield('content')

Has @isset($header) block for named header slot

Renders @include('layouts.navigation'), then slot content, then @stack('scripts')

Critical rule: any blade using <x-app-layout> must pass content as a slot (not sections). Any blade using @extends('layouts.app') + @section('content') will render blank, because there is no @yield('content') in that layout anymore.

layouts/navigation.blade.php
Shared nav. Uses $globalSettings for site name and header styling, and $headerMenuItems for the dynamic menu (set by AppServiceProvider, presumably).

7. CKEditor 5 setup
Installation
bash
npm install ckeditor5 @ckeditor/ckeditor5-style @ckeditor/ckeditor5-font \
  @ckeditor/ckeditor5-alignment @ckeditor/ckeditor5-highlight \
  @ckeditor/ckeditor5-list @ckeditor/ckeditor5-source-editing \
  @ckeditor/ckeditor5-show-blocks
Initializer — resources/js/ckeditor-init.js
First two lines are the essential CSS imports:

js
import 'ckeditor5/ckeditor5.css';
import 'ckeditor5/ckeditor5-content.css';
Without these, the editor renders with oversized unstyled icons.

Uses licenseKey: 'GPL' (valid for self-hosted GPL build)

Exposes window.initCkEditor(selector, options)

Merges options.simpleUpload into the config for image uploads

Attaches a form submit listener that syncs editor content back into the <textarea>

Full toolbar: undo/redo, sourceEditing, showBlocks, heading, style, fontSize, fontFamily, fontColor, fontBackgroundColor, bold, italic, underline, link, insertTable, highlight, blockQuote, codeBlock, alignment, lists, indent/outdent

Blade pattern (four files)
blade
@push('scripts')
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/build/assets/ckeditor-init-<HASH>.css">
<script type="module">
    import('{{ Vite::asset("resources/js/ckeditor-init.js") }}').then(() => {
        window.initCkEditor('#body', {
            simpleUpload: {
                uploadUrl: '{{ route("admin.posts.upload_image") }}',
                headers: { 'X-CSRF-TOKEN': '{{ csrf_token() }}' }
            }
        }).catch(err => console.error('[CKEditor] init failed', err));
    }).catch(err => console.error('[CKEditor] module load failed', err));
</script>
@endpush
Field targets:

File	Target
admin/pages/create.blade.php	#content
admin/pages/edit.blade.php	#content
admin/posts/create.blade.php	#body
admin/posts/edit.blade.php	#body
Why <link> plus dynamic import()
Vite splits dynamically imported modules into separate JS and CSS chunks. Loading only the JS leaves the CSS unloaded. The explicit <link> on the CSS chunk fixes it.

The <link> hash changes on every npm run build. After a rebuild, update the hash in all four blades.

Build output
public/build/assets/ckeditor-init-<HASH>.js (~1.1 MB)

public/build/assets/ckeditor-init-<HASH>.css (~219 KB)

Current hash at time of writing: ckeditor-init-BA9sK04e.css

vite.config.js
js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import laravel from 'laravel-vite-plugin';

export default defineConfig({
    plugins: [
        laravel({
            input: [
                'resources/css/app.css',
                'resources/js/app.js',
                'resources/js/ckeditor-init.js'
            ],
            refresh: true,
        }),
    ],
    optimizeDeps: { include: ['ckeditor5'] },
    build: { commonjsOptions: { include: [/ckeditor5/, /node_modules/] } },
});
8. Settings / theming
Settings page
admin/settings/index.blade.php. Form posts to PUT admin.settings.update. Fields:

settings[site_name] — string

settings[primary_color] — native color input, default #2563eb

settings[accent_color] — default #f59e0b

settings[text_color] — default #1f2937

settings[header_bg_color] — default #ffffff

settings[heading_font] — dropdown

settings[body_font] — dropdown

settings[border_radius] — dropdown

settings[container_width] — dropdown

header_image — file upload

remove_header_image — checkbox

How theming flows
Settings are stored in the settings table (key/value).

Both layouts read $settings in an @php block and output CSS variables in a <style> tag:

css
:root {
    --primary-color: ...;
    --accent-color: ...;
    --text-color: ...;
    --heading-font: ...;
    --body-font: ...;
    --border-radius: ...;
}
Both layouts now contain:

css
body {
    color: var(--text-color);
    font-family: var(--body-font);
}
h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 {
    font-family: var(--heading-font);
}
tailwind.config.js maps gray.700 and gray.900 to var(--text-color):

js
colors: {
    gray: {
        50:  '#f9fafb',
        100: '#f3f4f6',
        200: '#e5e7eb',
        300: '#d1d5db',
        400: '#9ca3af',
        500: '#6b7280',
        600: '#4b5563',
        700: 'var(--text-color)',
        800: '#1f2937',
        900: 'var(--text-color)',
        950: '#030712',
    },
}
Effect: changing text_color in settings updates body copy and headings site-wide (public and admin), because most copy uses text-gray-700 / text-gray-900.

Not wired up: primary_color, accent_color, border_radius, container_width are declared as CSS variables but no elements read them, except button[type="submit"] { background-color: var(--primary-color); } on admin pages.

9. AppServiceProvider (inferred)
I have not seen this file. From behavior, it shares two variables with all views:

$globalSettings — array of all settings

$headerMenuItems — menu items for the navigation

If you share this file at the start of a future session, the picture becomes complete.

10. Everything fixed during this project
Ordered roughly chronologically.

CKEditor invalid-license-key — trial key on the CDN wasn't valid. Solution: self-host GPL build via npm.

window.initCkEditor is not a function — the <script type="module" src> tag emitted by @vite was not executing in the browser. Replaced with an inline <script type="module"> doing a dynamic import() of the built file. Verified by testing the import from the console.

Stale compiled views in storage/framework/views/ — view:clear was sometimes not enough. Needed Remove-Item -Recurse -Force storage\framework\views\*.

Old UMD code still in blades — four editor blades had CDN-era code. Overwritten.

layouts/app.blade.php still referenced /js/ckeditor-init.js (a deleted file). Removed.

Blank pages on <x-app-layout> pages — layout used @yield('content'), but <x-app-layout> passes content as $slot. Changed to {{ $slot }} and added @isset($header).

CKEditor icons huge / toolbar vertical — Vite splits dynamic imports into JS + CSS chunks. Added explicit <link> on the CSS chunk in four blades.

$accentColor undefined — stale compiled view.

admin/posts/edit.blade.php loaded the pages edit form — referenced $page instead of $post. Overwritten.

Global text color setting did nothing — CSS variable declared but no rule used it. Added body { color: var(--text-color); } to both layouts and mapped Tailwind gray-700/gray-900 to the variable in tailwind.config.js.

Five admin blades broken — three used @extends('layouts.app') + @section('content') (blank after the {{ $slot }} change), and two used routes that don't exist (posts.* without the admin. prefix). Fixed:

admin/categories/edit.blade.php

admin/posts/create.blade.php

admin/posts/edit.blade.php

admin/posts/drafts.blade.php

admin/posts/show.blade.php

Post::$fillable had slug, but the table has no slug column — insert failed with SQLSTATE[HY000]: table posts has no column named slug. Removed slug from $fillable.

Post.php got wiped by a broken PowerShell command (a mistake on my side — used $content before defining it). Restored without BOM.

11. Working conventions and gotchas
File editing
Three create.blade.php files exist (pages/, posts/, categories/). Same for edit.blade.php. VS Code tabs show only the filename. Easy to edit the wrong file. Use Ctrl+P and type part of the path, or expand the folder in the sidebar and click from inside it.

VS Code saves have silently failed for this project — usually because the wrong file was open. Verify by running Get-Content <path> | Select-Object -First 5 after saving.

PowerShell
Set-Content -Encoding UTF8 writes a BOM. For .php files this causes Namespace declaration statement has to be the very first statement errors.
Use instead:

powershell
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $content, (New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding $false))
Blades/JSON/JS/CSS are fine with Set-Content.

PowerShell here-strings (@' ... '@) are fragile with spread syntax (...). Spread is dropped silently. Avoid it in files written this way.

Always add a verification step to file-writing commands. End with:

powershell
Write-Host "Written. Size: $((Get-Item $path).Length) bytes"
Verifying changes took
No output = nothing found. Select-String returning nothing means the pattern isn't there — could be because the fix worked or the file didn't change.

Compiled views hide blade changes. After editing a blade:

powershell
php artisan view:clear
If the change still doesn't appear:

powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force storage\framework\views\* -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
php artisan optimize:clear
Blank page debugging: View Page Source (Ctrl+U) first. php artisan serve logs may show nothing if PHP dies before Laravel's logger initializes. The service terminal itself prints the request line.

BOM check for app/
powershell
Get-ChildItem -Path app -Recurse -Filter *.php | ForEach-Object {
    $bytes = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($_.FullName)
    if ($bytes.Length -ge 3 -and $bytes[0] -eq 0xEF -and $bytes[1] -eq 0xBB -and $bytes[2] -eq 0xBF) {
        Write-Host "BOM found: $($_.FullName)"
    }
}
Empty output = clean.

